export const PROJECT_PROMPT = `Build an interactive website for affine alcove walks of type A₂.
Use coordinates x=<alpha_1,v>, y=<alpha_2,v>, with reflecting walls x=n, y=n, and x+y=n. The fundamental alcove A₀ satisfies x>0,y>0,x+y<1. Label the three walls of every alcove consistently as s₀,s₁,s₂, using green, red, and blue respectively. Colors must follow wall types in the affine Coxeter complex, rather than assigning one color to each family of parallel lines. The A₀ walls x+y=1,x=0,y=0 have types s₀,s₁,s₂ respectively.
In Free Drawing, each path starts at the yellow-highlighted A₀. Click a neighboring triangle to cross a shared wall or fold at any wall of the current alcove. Draw each fold as a turn back into that same alcove. Record folds and backtracking as steps. Let users draw several paths together in distinct colors reserved for paths, select one to edit, clear one path, or clear all paths. Draw direction arrows and step numbers. Use right multiplication w->ws_i for crossings. Include manual word input, a step log, Coxeter length and reduced-path checks, undo/redo, pan and zoom, recenter and fit controls, optional snapping to alcove centers, state restoration after refresh, and SVG export of the entire drawing.
Use https://cubicbear.github.io/doc/affineNotes.pdf#page=75 as a visual reference and Example 2.19 on page 19 for example paths. Clearly distinguish verified examples from demonstration routes. Keep mathematical logic separate from rendering, and provide unit tests for consistent wall types, adjacent-reflection involutions, the A₂ braid relation, Coxeter length, and undo/backtracking.
Technical goals: TypeScript + SVG, minimal runtime dependencies, and locally runnable source code, a README, and tests.`;
